fbsdkpi: signal_pending() and current_exiting() answer for the calling XNU thread

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT (this header and the added lines; the patched files keep
their FreeBSD/PureDarwin licences)

The PD LinuxKPI stubbed linux_signal_pending()/linux_fatal_signal_pending() and
linux_task_exiting() to false ("kernel threads on xnu take no signals"). Every Linux
interruptible wait loop that ends on signal_pending() (drm_syncobj_array_wait_timeout,
dma_fence_default_wait(intr), the Rust kernel crate's CondVar::wait_interruptible used
by the Asahi driver) therefore never ended for a dying or signalled client: XNU's
task_terminate aborts the thread, our THREAD_ABORTSAFE sleep returns at once, the loop
asks signal_pending(), gets false, and sleeps again -> the thread spins in the kernel
and the process stays in exit (SF-AC "a process that used Indium cannot exit", the
stuck-in-exit GPU helpers). A posted SIGTERM was swallowed the same way.

Now, for the calling thread only: true when its process is exiting (P_LEXIT, set by
exit1() before task_terminate_internal(); lock-free) or, where blocking is allowed,
when XNU has an unblocked, unignored signal posted to it (thread_issignal); fatal:
SIGKILL posted or exiting. Kernel threads (kernproc) still take no signals.
Uninterruptible waits stay uninterruptible: no GPU backing, mapping or job is freed
to make exit pass. -ERESTARTSYS still reaches user space as EINTR (layer 0012),
which libdrm's drmIoctl() retries.

Applies unchanged (fuzz 0) to the frozen CIO29 agx tree 0n94n35s... and dcp tree
gj14r67...; build-agx.nix / build-dcp.nix; host test test-waits.py.

